    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d156">

                <pb facs="0344" n="330" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg330"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00113 E.W./8–2644: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)<anchor n="57"
                        xml:id="d156-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-09-05T00:00:00-04:00">September 5,
                            1944—midnight</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7191. Re Embassy’s 6946 August 26, 6 p.m.<anchor n="58" xml:id="d156-f2"/> In
                    connection with Penrose’s<anchor n="59" xml:id="d156-f3"/> talks with
                        Coulson<anchor n="60" xml:id="d156-f4"/> it seems desirable that the
                    initiative on the question of discussions with the British and Soviets should
                    come from some source other than the United States. Accordingly it is felt that
                    you should not press further for an early conference but wait for such a
                    suggestion to come from the British or some other government. This is not
                    intended to alter any of the views expressed in the Department’s 6485 of August
                    16, and you should, in your talks, be guided by that cable in all other
                    respects.</p>
                <p>The Department is agreeable to dealing immediately with problems of restitution
                    of identifiable objects as proposed by the British.</p>
                <p>Regarding economic security, a memorandum<anchor n="61" xml:id="d156-f5"/>
                    outlining the Department’s general position on the subject has been approved by
                    the Executive Committee on Economic Foreign Policy and is now to be discussed
                    with War and Navy Departments. This document will be sent to you as soon as at
                    least tentative agreement is reached.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d156-f1" n="57" type="source" xml:id="d156-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated as telegram 2136, September 5, 7 p.m., to Moscow.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d156-f2" n="58" xml:id="d156-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reviewed some of the general views of British Foreign Office
                    officials on the matter of reparations and asked for information regarding the
                    position of the Department’s work on economic security (740.00113
                    EW/8–2644).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d156-f3" n="59" xml:id="d156-f3-content">
                <p>Ernest F. Penrose, Adviser and Special Assistant to the Ambassador in the United
                    Kingdom.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d156-f4" n="60" xml:id="d156-f4-content">
                <p>John Eltringham Coulson, Acting First Secretary of the British Foreign Office,
                    Acting Head of the Economic Relations Department.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d156-f5" n="61" xml:id="d156-f5-content">
                <p>Dated August 14, <ref target="#pg278">p. 278</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
